    "problem": "Express $\\frac{22x-38}{(3-x)(2-x)(x+1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.",
    "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{22x-38}{(3-x)(2-x)(x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{(3-x)}+\\frac{B}{(2-x)}+\\frac{C}{(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(3-x)(2-x)(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n22x-38=A(2-x)(x+1)+B(3-x)(x+1)+C(3-x)(2-x).\n$$\n\nFor $x=3$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}22(3)-38 & =A(2-3)(3+1) \\\\  & \\,+B(3-3)(3+1) \\\\  & \\,+C(3-3)(2-3) \\\\ 66-38 & =A(-4) \\\\ A & =\\frac{28}{-4} \\\\ A & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}22(2)-38 & =A(2-2)(2+1) \\\\  & \\,+B(3-2)(2+1) \\\\  & \\,+C(3-2)(2-2) \\\\ 44-38 & =B(3) \\\\ B & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-1$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}22(-1)-38 & =A(2-(-1))(-1+1) \\\\  & \\,+B(3-(-1))(-1+1) \\\\  & \\,+C(3-(-1))(2-(-1)) \\\\ -22-38 & =C(12) \\\\ C & =\\frac{-60}{12} \\\\ C & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{22x-38}{(3-x)(2-x)(x+1)}=-\\frac{7}{(3-x)}+\\frac{2}{(2-x)}-\\frac{5}{(x+1)}.\n$$",
